//! D104 — `MFEM NC mesh v1.0` writer (polar-nc lane) regression pins.
//!
//! Round 104 added [`fem_io::mfem_nc`]: the non-conforming container writer
//! (`NCMesh::Print` + the curved-`nodes` tail of `Mesh::Printer`) plus the
//! hanging-free conforming fallback (`Mesh::Printer`'s plain `MFEM mesh v1.0`
//! branch), so the `polar-nc` miniapp can reproduce MFEM 4.10's products.
//!
//! Provenance of the two embedded goldens: generated by the **C++** MFEM
//! 4.10 `miniapps/meshing/polar-nc` (WSL, `$HOME/mfem410_ser`,
//! `polar-nc -no-vis -n 2` and `-n 1`), copied verbatim from
//! `fem-rs/tmp/d104pnc/gold/`.  The round-104 byte-diff ledger showed the
//! Rust miniapp's full output byte-identical to the C++ products for **21/21**
//! parameter sets (def, ±sfc, -o 1..5, -n 1/2/3/4/7/20, -a 0.4/0.5/2,
//! -phi 30/120/270, -r 0.5); the two smallest artifacts are embedded here so
//! the *writer* half of that equality stays pinned in CI (the generator half
//! lives in the miniapp binary and is re-verified against WSL MFEM by the
//! round script).  The GridFunction values below are transcribed from the
//! C++ text; `format_g` renders 8-significant-digit literals identically
//! after an f64 round trip, so transcription (not derivation) is exact.
//!
//! The tests were written against these bytes *before* the final writer state
//! was reached (red): during development they caught a missing
//! `nvert + nedge` interior-dof base and a doubled blank line before the
//! `nodes` section — the teeth are real.

use fem_io::mfem::{read_mfem, MfemFile};
use fem_io::mfem_nc::{
    write_conforming_mesh_v1, write_nc_mesh, ConformingMeshV1, NcBoundary, NcElement, NcGeometry,
    NcMeshV1,
};

